<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Madagascar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> was the Allied campaign of 1942 to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> French Madagascar and deny its harbours to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. British forces landed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antsiranana">Diego Suarez</a> on 5 May 1942 and captured the naval base, then advanced on the rest of the island from September. The Vichy governor-general surrendered in November, and the island was handed over to the Free French.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-madagascar.jpg" alt="Battle of Madagascar" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: British Official Photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madagascar_KAR_1942.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Diego-Suarez, a large bay with a fine harbour at the northern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> signed by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-iii">Ranavalona III</a> on 17 December 1885. In March 1942 Japanese carriers raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trincomalee">Trincomalee</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> and drove the British Eastern Fleet back to a new base at Kilindini Harbour, Mombasa. The British feared that Japanese submarines, some with a range of more than 10,000 miles, might use Madagascar and threaten Allied shipping from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> at first doubted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had the resources, but by 12 March 1942 he had agreed that planning should begin. The Free French were explicitly excluded, after the experience at Dakar in 1940. Force 121 under Major-General Robert Sturges and a naval force under Rear-Admiral Edward Syfret assembled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Operation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ironclad">Ironclad</a> began on 5 May 1942 with landings at Courrier Bay and Ambararata Bay, west of Diego-Suarez. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> forces under Governor General Armand Léon Annet had about 8,000 troops, most of them Malagasy. The landings met little resistance, but a frontal assault on the Joffre line near the naval base failed on 6 May, and ten of twelve tanks were lost. The deadlock was broken when the destroyer HMS Anthony ran past the harbour defences and landed fifty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-marines">Royal Marines</a> in the town. Antisarane surrendered that evening. The British lost 109 killed and 283 wounded in three days, the French about 700 casualties.</p><p>On 29 May Japanese midget submarines reached Diego-Suarez and damaged the battleship HMS Ramillies. The rest of the island was taken in Operations Stream, Line and Jane, with landings at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahajanga">Majunga</a> on 10 September and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toamasina">Tamatave</a> on 18 September; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Tananarive</a> fell on 23 September. An armistice was signed at Ambalavao on 6 November, and Annet surrendered two days later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>This was the first large Allied operation combining sea, land and air forces, and it put the island's deep-water ports beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a>. Free French General Paul Legentilhomme became High Commissioner in December 1942. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> later saw the Malagasy Uprising of 1947 to 1948 and became independent on 26 June 1960.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Madagascar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Second World War Northern Rhodesian units fought for Britain in the East African Campaign, the <b>Battle of Madagascar</b> and Burma, and the Ministry of Supply bought the mines' copper for the armed forces at set prices.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It remained a major naval base, captured by British forces in 1942 during the <b>Battle of Madagascar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/diego-suarez-colony">Diego-Suarez Colony</a></li></ul></section>
